Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SMALL ROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR (mid-30s, gaunt, intense eyes, dressed in simple, worn clothes) sits on a wooden stool in a stark, windowless room. He looks directly at the camera, a manic energy barely contained.

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! And observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The NARRATOR's eyes narrow, a flicker of obsession.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A dark, opulent bedroom. An OLD MAN (70s, frail, sleeping soundly) lies in a large four-poster bed.

The NARRATOR, dressed in dark clothes, stands at the bedroom door. He slowly, painstakingly, turns the latch. The movement is agonizingly slow, silent.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work! I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

The NARRATOR pushes the door open a fraction of an inch. He holds a dark lantern, completely closed, no light escaping. He thrusts his head into the narrow opening.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Oh, you would have laughed to see how cunningly I thrust it in! I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man’s sleep. It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

The NARRATOR's head is now fully in the room. He carefully, slowly, UNDOES the lantern. A faint CREAK of hinges. He freezes, then continues. A single, thin ray of light escapes, falling directly onto the OLD MAN'S face.

The OLD MAN'S eye is closed. It's a pale blue, but the "film" is not visible while closed.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - EIGHTH NIGHT

The NARRATOR is at the door again, even more cautious. His movements are painfully slow.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

He pushes the door open. A faint, almost imperceptible CHUCKLE escapes his lips.

The OLD MAN stirs in bed, a sudden, startled movement.

OLD MAN
(Muttering, groggy)
Who’s there?

The NARRATOR freezes, utterly still. He holds his breath. The room is pitch black, shutters fastened tight.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening;—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A low, stifled GROAN comes from the OLD MAN. It's not pain, but deep terror.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well.

The NARRATOR's face, barely visible in the darkness, shows a grim satisfaction.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. I knew that he had been lying awake ever since the first slight noise, when he had turned in the bed. His fears had been ever since growing upon him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS

The NARRATOR, still holding the closed lantern, waits. The silence is heavy, broken only by the OLD MAN's shallow, ragged breathing.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
When I had waited a long time, very patiently, without hearing him lie down, I resolved to open a little—a very, very little crevice in the lantern.

He slowly, painstakingly, slides the lantern's cover. A tiny, spider-thread ray of light shoots out. It finds its target.

The OLD MAN'S eye. Wide, wide open. Pale blue, with a hideous, milky film over it. It seems to stare directly at the NARRATOR.

The NARRATOR's face contorts with fury.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones; but I could see nothing else of the old man’s face or person: for I had directed the ray as if by instinct, precisely upon the damned spot.

A low, dull, quick THUMP-THUMP-THUMP begins. Like a watch wrapped in cotton. It's the OLD MAN'S HEARTBEAT.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

The heartbeat grows louder, quicker. THUMP-THUMP-THUMP-THUMP. The NARRATOR's eyes are wide, fixed on the eye. He scarcely breathes.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
But even yet I refrained and kept still. I scarcely breathed. I held the lantern motionless. I tried how steadily I could maintain the ray upon the eye. Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

The sound is now a frantic, pounding rhythm. The NARRATOR's face is a mask of terror and rage.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I have told you that I am nervous: so I am. And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror.

The heartbeat is deafening. The NARRATOR CLAPS his hands over his ears, but the sound is internal, inescapable.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man’s hour had come!

With a loud YELL, the NARRATOR throws open the lantern, flooding the room with light. He LEAPS into the room.

The OLD MAN lets out a single, piercing SHRIEK.

The NARRATOR drags the OLD MAN from the bed to the floor. He pulls the heavy bed over him. The SHRIEK cuts off abruptly.

The NARRATOR smiles, a grotesque, triumphant grin.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This, however, did not vex me; it would not be heard through the wall. At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

The NARRATOR removes the bed. He kneels, places his hand on the OLD MAN'S chest. He waits. No pulsation.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
He was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - PREDawn

The room is a scene of methodical horror. The NARRATOR works hastily, but silently.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body. The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

We see glimpses: a tub catching something, the NARRATOR lifting floorboards. He replaces the planks with meticulous care. The floor looks undisturbed.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A distant CLOCK CHIMES FOUR TIMES.

INT. HOUSE - FRONT DOOR - DAWN

A loud, insistent KNOCKING at the street door.

The NARRATOR, now composed, dressed neatly, walks to the door with a light, confident step. He opens it.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o’clock—still dark as midnight. As the bell sounded the hour, there came a knocking at the street door. I went down to open it with a light heart,—for what had I now to fear?

Three POLICE OFFICERS stand on the stoop: OFFICER REYNOLDS (stern, older), OFFICER SMITH (young, observant), and OFFICER JONES (burly, quiet).

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Good morning, sir. We are officers of the police. A shriek was reported by a neighbour during the night.

NARRATOR
(Smiling warmly)
Gentlemen, welcome. Please, come in. The shriek, I assure you, was my own in a dream. The old man, my dear friend, is absent in the country.

He gestures them inside with an expansive sweep of his arm.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

The NARRATOR leads the officers into the OLD MAN'S bedroom. The room is tidy, the bed made. The floorboards are seamless.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
I took my visitors all over the house. I bade them search—search well. I led them, at length, to his chamber. I showed them his treasures, secure, undisturbed.

The officers glance around, their eyes scanning the room. Nothing seems amiss.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues, while I myself, in the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

The NARRATOR brings three chairs for the officers, then pulls a fourth chair for himself. He positions it precisely over the spot where the body is hidden. He sits, beaming.

The officers sit, exchanging pleasantries. OFFICER SMITH's eyes linger on the NARRATOR for a moment.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A quiet night, otherwise?

NARRATOR
(Cheerily)
Quite! A most peaceful slumber, once my dream had passed.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease. They sat, and while I answered cheerily, they chatted of familiar things.

The NARRATOR's smile begins to falter. He subtly rubs his temples.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted.

A faint, rhythmic THUMP-THUMP-THUMP begins, almost imperceptible, like a distant, muffled drum. The NARRATOR's eyes dart around the room, then back to the officers.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct:—it continued and became more distinct: I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling: but it continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The NARRATOR's voice grows louder, more animated, almost frantic. The officers continue to chat, seemingly oblivious.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS

The THUMP-THUMP-THUMP is now clearly audible, growing steadily louder. The NARRATOR is visibly pale, gasping for breath.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
No doubt I now grew very pale;—but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased—and what could I do? It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not.

He rises, pacing the floor with heavy, agitated strides. His gestures become wild.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased. I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased. Why would they not be gone?

The heartbeat is a relentless, pounding rhythm, filling the room. The officers still chat, smiling politely.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Oh God! what could I do? I foamed—I raved—I swore! I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased. It grew louder—louder—louder!

The NARRATOR seizes his chair and slams it down, then drags it across the floor, a SCREECHING sound. The heartbeat continues, louder than ever.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!—this I thought, and this I think.

The NARRATOR's eyes are wide with terror and rage. He points a trembling finger at the floor.

NARRATOR
(Screaming)
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers stare, stunned, as the NARRATOR collapses, pointing wildly at the floorboards. The pounding HEARTBEAT fills the silence.

INT. SMALL ROOM - DAY

The NARRATOR is back on his stool, slumped, exhausted. The manic energy is gone, replaced by a hollow despair. He looks directly at the camera.

NARRATOR
(Quietly, defeated)
Anything was better than this agony! Anything was more tolerable than this derision! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die! And now—again!—hark! Louder! Louder! Louder! Louder!

He closes his eyes, the sound of the beating heart still echoing in his mind.

FADE OUT.
